#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515639} :activity/id #uuid "7667169e-9de4-503b-b68f-ed58d102783f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048346 :topic/id #uuid "977d7a77-a9aa-53d4-9b35-0ca8240c298e" :topic/math-academy-id 4124} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b2db6b-e4a2-59ce-aec1-ef7d698cc557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514912} {:answer/id #uuid "8592fd8a-a60b-5222-be37-08c4d12b699e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515149} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3abf38-e119-56f0-b466-68f90093ef89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515236} {:answer/id #uuid "e77a7ff1-b917-56d9-9450-beaa5bf0254b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515359} {:answer/id #uuid "fe364573-b5d5-5e45-b831-a1dc4fd75f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515408}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515408} :answer-field/id #uuid "50b0880a-d63a-547c-ac9e-9646cff1bb56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "871ccc09-e09c-5033-9027-58208490bfda" :question/math-academy-id "q-159842" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=4,\\,{x}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =1!\\cdot 4!\\cdot 2!=1\\cdot 24\\cdot 2=48, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1+4+2=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-3\\theta +7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-3+7+48=52.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "23769111-4f78-5f80-a5ce-c23eb81061b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "913d6877-2fb9-5a5f-9371-e035641a5329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191051} {:answer/id #uuid "152bacff-aa47-51ea-a7b5-7fb58840923c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "107" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191052} {:answer/id #uuid "3bee2cb3-faff-53e8-9928-f4fec6cc836f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191053} {:answer/id #uuid "59d6da0c-b14e-535c-8319-e8654bb669b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191054} {:answer/id #uuid "664dde07-5616-526b-8779-8ff7d8c12b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186191053} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed289314-c60f-59bd-b54d-8d68f69bcf50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d142c298-92b6-5eb8-b79c-25b9f7c9fa90" :question/math-academy-id "q-159772" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=4,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =4!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 2!=24\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2=96, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =4+2+1+2=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-4\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+9+96=101.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2cd8a19e-1f39-5c47-b2a8-8e4d001f8902" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047ee7a4-18a8-58d6-b450-3ca4a9d4d7e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514881} {:answer/id #uuid "10c4fde8-8a2c-5044-81db-2ef8e8bc14ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514909} {:answer/id #uuid "3a95cbe7-f451-5320-9372-bdc4d36f1770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515000} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2b2632-3760-5a09-ae52-b564a7addc6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515271} {:answer/id #uuid "c562738c-9074-56f9-991a-6abc27300f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515294}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514881} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee53ea70-06f7-5a83-81c3-442de950c369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6141f906-6242-52a2-92a1-6fa0f7b0342a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159839" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=3,\\,{x}_{3}=2,\\,{x}_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =1!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 2!=1\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=24, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1+3+2+2=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-4\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+8+24=28.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "36d19675-a010-5c2a-8793-7b1c7dc46266" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270a20eb-2f96-518b-8d8b-0a6f14a6f2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514962} {:answer/id #uuid "33016b67-c47d-53ac-b94c-da423f6e0a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514986} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5ba770-0516-5a66-b241-3645843d77b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515169} {:answer/id #uuid "939d4573-0cca-5f59-8d62-b98251fd1a56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515183} {:answer/id #uuid "e8108738-f541-569a-ad5c-f71133abad7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514986} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcaa65fb-24e7-5e34-ae1b-a2d7eaa873aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6aba45a4-2e2c-520c-97f5-9345f709dabd" :question/math-academy-id "q-159898" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(3,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=2,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=3,\\,{x}_{4}=1,\\,{x}_{5}=1.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=3$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{3-x},\\,x=0,1,2,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(3-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(3-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(3n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{4}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{5}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(3n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3\\cdot 1\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =81,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=2+1+3+1+1=8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(81)+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(3\\cdot 5-8)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(81)+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=81+8+7=96.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "88bd62ec-15d2-5a2e-93d2-79206567236d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3285b65f-b9c0-5ba9-bc03-8954f9ecc650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,604" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514985} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7e44dd-ef64-5fe5-bc02-5fd88cecfb82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,764" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515009} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b9444d-658f-5c5d-b85f-1d9c6267f666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,818" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515216} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2a7909-54ad-5cbd-9e86-98ef7dc072d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515270} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c934c7-75c4-510e-9d9c-2fe6ca8393ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,846" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515216} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6d8322b-8874-5773-8226-90a9cc0dfa28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bde496f4-3c68-52cf-9034-88a484da871a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159891" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(6,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=4,\\,{x}_{2}=3,\\,{x}_{3}=5.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{6-x},\\,x=0,1,…,6.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=6$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{6-x},\\,x=0,1,…,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(6-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(6-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(6n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(6n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5}) \\\\  & =15\\cdot 20\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1\\,800,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=4+3+5=12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\,800)+12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(6\\cdot 3-12)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1\\,800+12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1\\,800+12+6=1\\,818.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bd709fd0-dfec-5ed5-b548-5cd3d4707f29" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515665}}] :activity/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Discrete Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514857}]}
